{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\nowidctlpar\f0\fs24 Mr. Esposito reviewed the agenda item and staff recommendation. He noted recent updates to the Congestion Management LOPP, explaining that several higher-cost widening projects on county roads were removed so that more feasible projects could advance given the limited funding available for the state and federal network. Mr. Esposito also reminded the MPO Board that the Hernando-Citrus MPO receives only about $510,000 annually in Transportation Alternatives (TA) funding, which increases the need to carefully evaluate TA projects. \par
\par
\b\i Motion: \b0\i0 A motion was made by Vice Chair Bays to remove Priority #35, Ft. Island Trail Multi-Use Trail, from the TA List of Priority Projects. The motion was seconded by Councilman Holmes and the motion passed 7-0.\b \b0\par
\pard\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\nowidctlpar\f0\fs24 Mr. Esposito reviewed the agenda item and staff recommendation, then introduced Wally Blaine, the MPO General Planning Consultant with Benesch & Associates. Mr. Blaine presented an overview of the funding included in the FY2027-FY2031 TIP, noting that indirect costs are now reflected in the five-year program. He also stated that, aside from Turnpike funding, since the final phase of the Suncoast Parkway was funded in the previous five-year program, overall funding levels have remained relatively consistent with the prior program.\b\i\f1\par
\par
\b0\i0\f0 In response to Commissioner Bays\rquote inquiry, Suzanne Ziegler, District\u8239?7 Liaison with the Florida Department of Transportation, stated that Districts\u8239?7 and\u8239?5 are working collaboratively to coordinate the upcoming reconfiguration of the bridge and the road-widening project for SR\u8239?200. She encouraged the MPO Board and staff to monitor the project\rquote s priority and placement during the next 2055 Long-Range Transportation Plan (LRTP) update and adjust the priority if needed. Chair Allocco recommended that Citrus County consider reserving Right-of-Way during development approvals to accommodate the future widening. Ms. Ziegler noted that a Project Development and Environment (PD&E) study is available, which outlines the commitments required for the project, including Right-of-Way needs.\par
\pard\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\nowidctlpar\f0\fs24 Mr. Esposito reviewed the agenda item, staff recommendation, and introduced Marc Ispass, the MPO General Planning Consultant with Kimley-Horn & Associates. Mr. Ispass provided a PowerPoint presentation outlining the Context Classification framework and the metrics used to determine Level of Service (LOS) analysis for both Hernando and Citrus counties. He explained that LOS analyses are important because they support local jurisdictions during development review and establish baseline traffic performance measures. \par
\pard\cf1\f1\par
}

